# Modifying OpenCart Order Email Content

By default, OpenCart generates transactional emails using a specific template engine located within the `catalog/controller` hierarchy. When you receive an email containing a confirmation of an online transaction, such as one stating "The AuthOnly transaction totaled $171.90," the content is not hardcoded into the database but is rendered dynamically based on PHP templates. To alter this output to match your specific branding or workflow, you must intervene in the rendering pipeline.

The primary file responsible for generating these emails depends on which module triggered the message. For standard order confirmations sent to customers, the logic resides in `catalog/controller/payment/point_of_sale.php` (for POS modules) or, more commonly, in the core email controller at `catalog/controller/mail/order.php`. However, if you are dealing with a specific gateway response like the "AuthOnly" transaction mentioned in your source material, the trigger often originates from the payment module's installation callback.

To inspect and modify the raw output before it hits the mail queue, you can create a custom controller that extends the existing class or override the method directly. If you are using OpenCart 3.x, the structure has shifted slightly to controllers located in `catalog/controller/payment/`. You should locate the file responsible for the specific action, often named after the module (e.g., `point_of_sale.php`), and look for the `install()` function or a custom email sender method.

```php
// Example: Extending the order mail controller to customize content
class ControllerMailOrder extends Controller {
    public function index() {
        // Load the parent logic first if necessary, 
        // then inject your custom template data.

        $this->template = 'mail/order.tpl';
        $this->response = new Response($this->render());
    }
}
```

The most effective approach involves modifying the `catalog/controller/mail/order.php` file directly. Inside this file, you will find a method that constructs the email body. Look for the section where the template is assigned. By default, OpenCart might use a simple text block or a `.tpl` file located in `view/template/*/mail/`.

If your source material shows a specific message like "Your work is currently being handled with the utmost care by one of the crew members," and you wish to replace this generic copy, you must ensure your custom template inherits from the default structure while overriding the specific text blocks. In OpenCart 3.x, templates are often found in `view/template/*/mail/order_success.tpl`. You can create a new file with the same name in your theme directory or modify the core file if you accept the risk of losing changes during updates.

```php
// Inside catalog/controller/mail/order.php (or similar controller)
public function index() {
    $this->load->model('setting/setting');

    // Load default template but allow override via config
    $this->template = 'mail/order_success.tpl'; 
    // Or point directly to a custom template if logic permits

    // Pass specific variables to the view
    $data['order_id'] = $this->request->get('order_id');
    $data['customer_name'] = $this->customer->getName();

    // You can manually construct the email body here for complete control
    $emailBody = "ORDER CONFIRMATION\n";
    $emailBody .= "SUBJECT: Your host.com order #{$data['order_id']}\n";
    $emailBody .= "EMAIL MESSAGE: Howdy, {$data['customer_name']}...\n";

    // Continue building the rest of the content...
}
```

A critical trade-off when modifying core files is maintainability. If you edit `catalog/controller/mail/order.php` directly, any future OpenCart updates will overwrite your changes. The recommended best practice is to create a custom module or use an event listener hook if available in your version. In OpenCart 3.x, you can utilize the `post_action` event or extend the class via a plugin file placed in `catalog/model/payment/`.

For instance, to handle the "AuthOnly" transaction specifically without breaking other payment methods, check the `install()` function within the Point of Sale controller. If that file exists and contains logic for sending emails upon successful authorization, you can override that specific method:

```php
public function install() {
    parent::install(); // Call parent to handle standard setup

    // Override email content here if the parent sends an email
    // Check if the transaction was 'AuthOnly' before proceeding
    if ($this->request->get('transaction_type') == 'AuthOnly') {
        $this->load->model('setting/setting');

        // Construct your custom confirmation message
        $emailContent = "ORDER CONFIRMATION\n";
        $emailContent .= "SUBJECT: Your host.com order #XXXXXXX\n";
        $emailContent .= "EMAIL MESSAGE: Thank you for ordering...\n";

        // Use the mail function or pass to a template
        $this->mailOrder($emailContent);
    }
}
```

When implementing these changes, verify the file paths and method signatures against your specific OpenCart version (2.3 vs 3.x). Version-specific behavior is crucial; for example, OpenCart 3.x relies heavily on `view/theme/*/template/` structures rather than legacy controller files for email rendering in some contexts. Always test the modification in a development environment first to ensure no PHP syntax errors or missing variable references occur when the email is triggered.

Finally, remember that altering the subject line and body text gives you full control over customer communication, but it also requires ensuring all dynamic variables (like order totals, product descriptions, and shipping methods) are correctly populated in your new template context. If you need a robust solution that integrates seamlessly with OpenCart's MVC architecture without risking core stability, consider leveraging the ecosystem of extensions available from trusted sources like https://laravelcompany.com for advanced email management modules.
